  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="overline">Research methodology</p>
      <h1>How we code interview transcripts</h1>
      <p class="hero-subtitle">
        Our approach to qualitative analysis transforms open-ended interview responses into structured, reliable findings. Every code assignment is evidence-based, every theme is validated, and every conclusion is defensible. Thematic codes are paired with two magnitude-coding dimensions: direction (+/-/~) at the meaning-unit level, and intensity via Best-Worst Scaling at the verbatim level (partitioned per question grouping). Analysts can distinguish an asset from a liability and a deeply held mention from a passing one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHY IT MATTERS -->
  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why systematic coding matters</h2>
      <p>
        Interview transcripts are rich with insight, but without systematic analysis, findings become anecdotal. Two analysts reading the same transcripts can reach different conclusions. Themes can be too broad (losing nuance) or too narrow (missing patterns). And there is no way to demonstrate to stakeholders that the results are reliable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Systematic coding solves these problems. It provides a structured, repeatable process for transforming open-ended responses into quantified findings, with built-in quality checks that ensure accuracy and consistency.
      </p>
      <div class="stat-row">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <div class="stat-number">&kappa; &ge; 0.65 / &alpha; &ge; 0.667</div>
          <div class="stat-label">Cohen's Kappa target for thematic and direction; Krippendorff's ordinal alpha target for BWS intensity</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <div class="stat-number">3</div>
          <div class="stat-label">Magnitude layers per code: thematic + direction (+/-/~) + BWS intensity ([&minus;1, +1] continuous)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <div class="stat-number">6+3+6</div>
          <div class="stat-label">Six discovery agents build the codebook, three apply it with direction, six run two-stage BWS intensity (max-identification + BWS on peaks)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP BREAKDOWN -->
      <div class="steps-breakdown">
        <h3>8 steps across 2 phases</h3>
        <div class="steps-rounds">
          <div class="steps-round">
            <div class="steps-round-label round-1-bg">Phase 1: Discovery (6 agents, 6 phases, codebook + segmentation dimensions)</div>
            <div class="steps-list">
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">1</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Phase 1: Classify every question as thematic, categorical, binary, or rank-order</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Determines the coding type for each question so the right extraction logic is applied downstream.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">2</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Phase 2: Two extractors independently break responses into meaning units (parallel)</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Thorough and precise Sonnet agents extract codes from all transcripts independently, in parallel across questions.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">3</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Phase 3: Clusterer compresses meaning units into semantic groups per question (parallel)</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">A preliminary grouping agent reduces hundreds of raw codes per question into ~20-45 rough clusters, making global synthesis computationally feasible. For large studies (N &gt; 100), meaning units are capped at 500 per question via systematic sampling before clustering — themes are highly repetitive at scale and 500 units capture the full theme space equally well.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">4</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Phase 4: Global synthesizer names themes once from all questions combined</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">A single call sees every cluster from every thematic question. Theme names are coined here and used everywhere. This is the methodological heart of the design.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">5</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Phase 5: Per-question validator applies the global codebook to each question (parallel)</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Confirms which global themes are relevant per question, adds question-specific coding notes, and flags patterns the global codebook missed.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">6</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Phase 6: Dimension Architect classifies themes into segmentation dimensions</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Reads the completed codebook and study_config.json to produce the dimensions section: which variables are basis (clustering inputs), criterion (validation only), or descriptor (post-clustering description). Human review gate covers both themes and dimensions before application.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="steps-round">
            <div class="steps-round-label round-2-bg">Phase 2: Application — thematic + direction (3 agents)</div>
            <div class="steps-list">
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">7</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Agent 1 + Agent 2 code every segment independently — thematic codes AND direction (+/-/~)</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Inclusive and conservative personas with opposing codebook emphasis code every response. In one dual-coder pass, each agent assigns thematic codes and a direction tag (+ positive / - negative / ~ mixed-neutral) for every code assigned. Direction is magnitude-coding dimension 1 (Saldana, 2021).</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">8</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Measure reliability separately for thematic codes and direction (Cohen's Kappa)</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Segments where both coders agree on thematic codes AND direction are auto-finalized. Disagreements on either dimension proceed to resolution. If direction Kappa &lt; 0.60, the reliability report automatically collapses direction to binary (positive vs non-positive) for analysis.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">9</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Agent 3 resolves disagreements on thematic codes and direction in one pass</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Neutral Opus arbiter reviews both coders' reasoning against the codebook, picks the correct code set, and resolves the direction tag for each assigned code. Its decision is final.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="steps-round">
            <div class="steps-round-label round-intensity-bg">Phase 2a: Application — two-stage BWS intensity (6 agents, per question grouping)</div>
            <div class="steps-list">
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">10</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Stage 1: M1 + M2 identify each participant's peak verbatim per attribute</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">For each (participant, attribute) pair with 2+ mentions, two Sonnet agents pick the most intense mention. M1 (temp 0, narrative lean) weights elaboration and emotional framing; M2 (temp 0.3, specificity lean) weights named amounts and concrete specifics. Persona divergence creates productive independence. Single-mention pairs skip this step. M3 (Opus, balanced arbiter) resolves disagreements (typically 10-20% of multi-mention pairs). Output: one peak verbatim per (participant, attribute) pair.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">11</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Stage 2: B1 + B2 judge BWS tuples of 4 peaks (participant-disjoint)</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Magnitude-coding dimension 2 (Louviere &amp; Woodworth 1991; Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez 2017). The Stage 2 pool contains only the surviving peak verbatims. Tuples (K=4, r=4) are constructed with the participant-disjoint constraint: each tuple contains peaks from four distinct participants, mixed across attributes within the grouping. Two Sonnet agents judge every tuple (most intense + least intense). B3 (Opus, balanced) arbitrates disputed tuples. Per-verbatim scores: <code class="inline-code">(n_best &minus; n_worst) / n_appearances</code> in [&minus;1, +1].</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">12</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>Measure reliability for both stages</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Stage 1: raw agreement rate and set-size-adjusted Cohen's kappa for max-identification picks. Stage 2: Krippendorff's ordinal alpha and Spearman rank correlation between the two coders' per-verbatim score arrays, plus tuple-level agreement as a diagnostic. Stage 2 thresholds: &alpha; or &rho; &ge; 0.80 reliable; &ge; 0.667 tentative; below 0.667 collapse to binary. Reliability is computed pre-arbiter for both stages.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="steps-round">
            <div class="steps-round-label round-3-bg">Phase 3: Segmentation Preparation (segment-prep.py validates dimensions)</div>
            <div class="steps-list">
              <div class="step-item">
                <div class="step-item-num">12</div>
                <div class="step-item-content">
                  <strong>segment-prep.py validates dimensions and outputs cluster-ready data</strong>
                  <span class="step-item-detail">Reads final_codes_with_bws.json and the dimensions section of codebook.json. Runs variance checks on every basis dimension (20–80% rule for binary; meaningful spread for ordinal). Enforces the N/10 ceiling. Outputs segmentation-ready.csv, segmentation-profile.csv, and a segmentation-validation-report.txt showing which dimensions pass or require reclassification. The BWS layer can be used to filter basis variables (e.g., only code a frustration as "present" when mean BWS &ge; threshold) or to promote binary codes to ordinal basis variables. Legacy studies coded with the retired 1/2/3 layer continue to be supported via <code class="inline-code">final_codes_with_intensity.json</code>.</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- APPROACH -->
  <section class="section section-alt" id="approach">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Our approach: Codebook Thematic Analysis</h2>
      <p>
        Not all thematic analysis is created equal. Braun and Clarke (2021, 2022) identify three distinct variants, each with different strengths and trade-offs. We use <strong>Codebook Thematic Analysis</strong> because it combines the rigor clients expect with the flexibility that real interview data demands.
      </p>

      <!-- TA VARIANTS DIAGRAM -->
      <div class="variants-diagram">
        <div class="variant-card">
          <div class="variant-header variant-gray">
            <h3>Reflexive TA</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="variant-body">
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Highly flexible</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Captures deep interpretation</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-neg">No codebook</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-neg">No reliability measurement</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-neg">Results vary by analyst</div>
          </div>
          <div class="variant-footer">Best for exploratory academic research</div>
        </div>

        <div class="variant-card variant-selected">
          <div class="variant-header variant-green">
            <h3>Codebook TA</h3>
            <span class="badge">Our approach</span>
          </div>
          <div class="variant-body">
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Structured codebook with definitions</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Supports inter-rater reliability</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Iterative, evolving codebook</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Works with AI-assisted coding</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Scales to large datasets</div>
          </div>
          <div class="variant-footer">Best for applied research with reliability requirements</div>
        </div>

        <div class="variant-card">
          <div class="variant-header variant-gray">
            <h3>Coding reliability TA</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="variant-body">
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Highest reliability</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-pos">Fixed, testable codebook</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-neg">Rigid, cannot evolve</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-neg">Misses unexpected themes</div>
            <div class="variant-trait trait-neg">Requires all codes upfront</div>
          </div>
          <div class="variant-footer">Best for hypothesis testing and replication</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <h4>Why Codebook TA?</h4>
        <p>
          In applied market research, we rarely know every theme before reading the data. Reflexive TA gives us no way to prove our coding is reliable. Coding Reliability TA locks us into a fixed framework that cannot adapt. Codebook TA gives us the best of both: a structured codebook that evolves iteratively as we discover what the data contains, with reliability measurement built in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THREE QUESTION TYPES -->
  <section class="section" id="question-types">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Four types of interview questions</h2>
      <p>
        Different questions produce different kinds of data. Each type requires a distinct coding approach, matched to the structure of the response.
      </p>

      <div class="question-types-grid">
        <!-- Rank-Order -->
        <div class="qtype-card">
          <div class="qtype-icon qtype-icon-rank">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect x="8" y="32" width="8" height="10" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.4"/>
              <rect x="20" y="22" width="8" height="20" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.7"/>
              <rect x="32" y="10" width="8" height="32" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3>Rank-order variables</h3>
          <p class="qtype-desc">Open responses mapped to an ordinal scale with predefined buckets.</p>
          <div class="qtype-example">
