              </tr>
              <tr class="taxonomy-native">
                <td><strong>Categorical</strong></td>
                <td>One label from 3+ unordered options</td>
                <td>Satisfaction coded as Satisfied / Mixed / Dissatisfied</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-native-badge">Native type</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="taxonomy-native">
                <td><strong>Rank-order</strong></td>
                <td>One bucket from an ordered scale</td>
                <td>Company size coded as 101-500 employees</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-native-badge">Native type</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="taxonomy-native">
                <td><strong>Binary</strong></td>
                <td>Yes/no, present/absent</td>
                <td>"Did they evaluate competitors?" coded as Yes</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-native-badge">Native type</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Sentiment / Valence</td>
                <td>Positive / neutral / negative attitude</td>
                <td>Tone toward vendor support coded as Negative</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-config-badge">Categorical</span> with Positive/Mixed/Negative options</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Frequency / Intensity</td>
                <td>How often or how strongly</td>
                <td>Dashboard usage coded as Daily / Weekly / Monthly</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-config-badge">Rank-order</span> with frequency buckets</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Temporal</td>
                <td>When something happened</td>
                <td>Started evaluating coded as Q3 2025</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-config-badge">Categorical</span> or <span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-config-badge">Rank-order</span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="taxonomy-rare">
                <td>Numeric / Continuous</td>
                <td>Extract an exact number</td>
                <td>Annual budget coded as $250,000</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-rare-badge">Rare</span> Use rank-order buckets instead. Verbal precision rarely warrants exact extraction.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="taxonomy-rare">
                <td>Multi-code Ordered</td>
                <td>Multiple ranked codes</td>
                <td>Top priorities coded as Cost (1st), Speed (2nd)</td>
                <td><span class="taxonomy-badge taxonomy-rare-badge">Rare</span> Use separate rank-order variables per item, or thematic without ranking.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MAGNITUDE CODING: DIRECTION + BWS INTENSITY -->
  <section class="section section-alt" id="magnitude">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Magnitude coding: direction + BWS intensity</h2>
      <p>
        Every thematic code carries two supplemental magnitude-coding dimensions (Saldana, 2021; Miles, Huberman &amp; Saldana, 2020): <strong>direction</strong> (the participant's evaluative stance) and <strong>intensity</strong> via Best-Worst Scaling (Louviere &amp; Woodworth, 1991; Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez, 2017). These dimensions answer the two questions marketing, product, and brand teams actually care about: "is this an asset or a liability?" and "how much weight is behind each mention?"
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h4>What this layer lets an analyst say</h4>
        <p>
          Without magnitude coding, the analyst can say <em>"62% of participants mentioned fees."</em> With magnitude coding, the analyst can say <em>"62% mentioned fees, direction negative, mean BWS intensity +0.31 within the current-pain-points question grouping, with 40% of mentions in the top intensity tier."</em> That second sentence is a behavioral signal. The first is a topic list.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="background: #fef3e7; border-color: #d4a843;">
        <h4>Why BWS replaced 1/2/3 ordinal rating in May 2026</h4>
        <p>
          The previous version of this pipeline asked coders to rate each (participant, code) pair on a 1/2/3 ordinal scale ("brief", "elaborated", "narrative"). Cohen's kappa typically landed at 0.30-0.40, below conventional reliability thresholds. This is consistent with the published norm for 3+ tier human ordinal coding (Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez, 2017): humans (and AI coders mirroring human conventions) are unreliable on absolute ordinal judgments, even with behavioral anchors. <strong>People are dramatically better at relative judgments than absolute ratings.</strong> BWS presents four verbatims at a time and asks the coder to pick the most and least intense within the tuple. Krippendorff's ordinal alpha on the resulting verbatim scores reliably lands above 0.6, often above 0.7. See the BWS methodology page for the full rationale and citations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="magnitude-grid">
        <!-- DIRECTION -->
        <div class="magnitude-card">
          <div class="magnitude-header magnitude-header-direction">
            <span class="magnitude-label">Dimension 1</span>
            <h3>Direction (+ / - / ~)</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="magnitude-body">
            <p class="magnitude-what">
              <strong>What it measures:</strong> the participant's evaluative stance toward the topic of the meaning unit. Is this positive, negative, or mixed/neutral?
            </p>
            <p class="magnitude-level">
              <strong>Level of analysis:</strong> meaning unit. Every meaning unit receives a direction tag paired with each assigned thematic code.
            </p>
            <p class="magnitude-when">
              <strong>When it runs:</strong> inside the dual-coder application pass, same prompt as thematic coding. No extra scripts, no extra API round-trips.
            </p>

            <div class="magnitude-table-wrap">
              <table class="magnitude-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th style="width: 60px;">Tag</th><th>Label</th><th>Observable criteria</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><span class="direction-pill direction-pos">+</span></td>
                    <td><strong>Positive</strong></td>
                    <td>Approval, satisfaction, praise, preference, or benefit. Markers: "great," "I like," "saves me time."</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><span class="direction-pill direction-neg">-</span></td>
                    <td><strong>Negative</strong></td>
                    <td>Disapproval, dissatisfaction, criticism, complaint, or cost. Markers: "terrible," "I hate," "cost me money."</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><span class="direction-pill direction-neutral">~</span></td>
                    <td><strong>Mixed/Neutral</strong></td>
                    <td>Factual statement, hedged evaluation, or both positive AND negative elements expressed together.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <div class="magnitude-example-wrap">
              <div class="magnitude-example-header">The same topic, opposite direction</div>
              <div class="magnitude-example-row">
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"I love their low fees"</span>
                <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="code-label">fees</span>
                <span class="direction-pill direction-pos">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="magnitude-example-row">
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"Their fees are outrageous"</span>
                <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="code-label">fees</span>
                <span class="direction-pill direction-neg">-</span>
              </div>
              <div class="magnitude-example-row">
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"They charge fees"</span>
                <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="code-label">fees</span>
                <span class="direction-pill direction-neutral">~</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="small-text" style="margin-top: 12px;">
              <strong>Default rule:</strong> when in doubt, code <code class="inline-code">~</code>. False positives in <code class="inline-code">+</code> and <code class="inline-code">-</code> are more harmful than over-use of <code class="inline-code">~</code>, because direction-driven analyses treat <code class="inline-code">+</code> and <code class="inline-code">-</code> as confident assignments.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- BWS INTENSITY -->
        <div class="magnitude-card">
          <div class="magnitude-header magnitude-header-intensity">
            <span class="magnitude-label">Dimension 2</span>
            <h3>BWS intensity ([&minus;1, +1])</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="magnitude-body">
            <p class="magnitude-what">
              <strong>What it measures:</strong> how strongly each thematic code is held in the data, measured at the verbatim level within each report-section question grouping. Each verbatim ends up with a continuous score from &minus;1 to +1; attribute-level intensity is the mean (plus top-tier rate and distribution shape) within the grouping.
            </p>
            <p class="magnitude-level">
              <strong>Level of analysis:</strong> verbatim, partitioned by question grouping. Intensity is question-relative; we never pool verbatims from different elicitation contexts (e.g., "what attracted you" vs "what frustrated you") in one BWS pass.
            </p>
            <p class="magnitude-when">
              <strong>When it runs:</strong> <code class="inline-code">run_bws_intensity.py</code> auto-chains after <code class="inline-code">run_coding.py</code> completes. For each question grouping declared in <code class="inline-code">study_config.json</code>, two AI coders judge a sparse balanced set of 4-verbatim tuples (K=4, r=4 appearances per verbatim). An Opus arbiter resolves disagreements.
            </p>

            <div class="magnitude-table-wrap">
              <table class="magnitude-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th style="width: 90px;">Output</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><span class="intensity-pill intensity-3" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">verbatim</span></td>
                    <td>Per-verbatim BWS score</td>
                    <td>Continuous score in [&minus;1, +1]. <code class="inline-code">(n_best &minus; n_worst) / n_appearances</code>. +1 = picked as most intense every time; &minus;1 = picked as least intense every time.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><span class="intensity-pill intensity-2" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">mean</span></td>
                    <td>Per-attribute mean</td>
                    <td>Average BWS score across all verbatims tagged with that code, within the question grouping. The strategic 2x2 chart plots this on the y-axis.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><span class="intensity-pill intensity-1" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">shape</span></td>
                    <td>Distribution shape</td>
                    <td>% of the attribute's verbatims that landed in the low / mid / high bands. Shows whether an attribute's intensity comes from a few standouts or uniformly weighted mentions.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <div class="magnitude-example-wrap">
              <div class="magnitude-example-header">A tuple the coder might see (all from "rejection reasons")</div>
              <div class="intensity-example">
                <span class="intensity-pill intensity-1" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">least</span>
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"They have higher fees."</span>
              </div>
              <div class="intensity-example">
                <span class="intensity-pill intensity-2" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">mid</span>
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"The fees were ridiculous, like $15 a month for nothing."</span>
              </div>
              <div class="intensity-example">
                <span class="intensity-pill intensity-2" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">mid</span>
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"App is glitchy."</span>
              </div>
              <div class="intensity-example">
                <span class="intensity-pill intensity-3" style="font-size:10px; padding: 3px 8px;">most</span>
                <span class="mu-text-sm">"Their customer service is awful. I waited 45 minutes on hold and got disconnected. When I called back the new rep had no record of my previous call."</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="small-text" style="margin-top: 12px;">
              <strong>The pick:</strong> coder selects "most intense" and "least intense" in the tuple. Two clicks per tuple. No anchor problem, no "what counts as a 2 vs a 3."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WHY THREE LEVELS, WHY NOT EMOTIONAL -->
      <div class="subsection">
        <h3>Design choices worth calling out</h3>
        <div class="design-choices">
          <div class="design-choice">
            <h4>Why every meaning unit gets a direction tag, even on question-directed responses</h4>
            <p>
              Meaning units are queried independently of their source question downstream (cross-question queries like "show me every meaning unit coded <code class="inline-code">fees</code>," segmentation inputs, database joins for verbatim drilldowns). When a meaning unit is retrieved outside its source-question context, the evaluative dimension is lost without an explicit direction tag. So every meaning unit gets one, regardless of whether the source question was framed to elicit positive or negative content.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="design-choice">
            <h4>Why BWS is partitioned by question grouping</h4>
            <p>
              Intensity is question-relative. Verbatims that come from a "what frustrated you" question are answering a different emotional prompt than verbatims from "what attracted you to this product." Frustration language is naturally more intense than praise language across most populations. Pooling them in one BWS pass would produce a bias artifact, not a finding. The right unit is the question grouping (one BWS pass per report section: drivers of selection, rejection reasons, current pain points, etc.). Each grouping's intensity scale stands on its own.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="design-choice">
            <h4>Why BWS uses all verbatims, not a sample</h4>
            <p>
              BWS scales to hundreds of items with a sparse balanced design. With N verbatims, tuple size K=4, and replication r=4 appearances per verbatim, total tuples = N. Every verbatim contributes to the final attribute-level scores; we do not subsample. The continuous-score output preserves all the within-attribute variation that a 3-tier collapse would lose.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="design-choice">
            <h4>Why direction over emotional coding</h4>
            <p>
              Emotional taxonomies (anger, joy, anxiety, etc.) require inferring internal states from text, which produces lower inter-rater agreement than direction coding (Krippendorff, 2018). Direction relies on observable linguistic markers. Emotion requires a layer of interpretation that compounds across coders and adds 6-20 categories per meaning unit — a gain in nuance that is not proportional to the reliability cost. Whether a brand association is favorable or unfavorable (direction) and how strongly held (intensity) drives the marketing recommendation; whether a negative association is anger vs. disappointment is interesting but rarely changes the action.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="design-choice">
            <h4>Reliability uses the right statistic for ordered data</h4>
            <p>
              Direction is a nominal three-class variable, so Cohen's Kappa is the right measure (target &kappa; &ge; 0.65; fallback collapse to binary at &lt; 0.60). BWS intensity produces ordered continuous scores, so the right measure is <strong>Krippendorff's ordinal alpha</strong> and <strong>Spearman rank correlation</strong> between the two coders' per-verbatim score arrays (target &alpha; &ge; 0.80; acceptable for tentative conclusions at &ge; 0.667; below 0.667 collapse to binary mean BWS &gt; 0 vs &le; 0). Cohen's unweighted kappa would be wrong for ordinal data because it treats all disagreements as equally serious, which understates agreement on a 1-3 scale.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="design-choice">
            <h4>The three codes per meaning unit</h4>
            <p>
              After all coding is complete, every meaning unit carries three layers:
            </p>
            <ol style="margin: 8px 0 0 20px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;">
              <li><strong>Thematic code</strong> — what topic it is about. Assigned at meaning-unit level by the dual-coder pass.</li>
              <li><strong>Direction</strong> — <code class="inline-code">+</code>, <code class="inline-code">-</code>, or <code class="inline-code">~</code>. Assigned at meaning-unit level by the same dual-coder pass.</li>
              <li><strong>BWS intensity</strong> — continuous score in [&minus;1, +1]. Assigned at verbatim level by <code class="inline-code">run_bws_intensity.py</code> within each question grouping. A verbatim can carry multiple BWS scores if it appears in multiple groupings.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- APPLIES TO B2B AND B2C -->
      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 32px;">
        <h4>Standard for every new study going forward</h4>
        <p>
          Magnitude coding applies to B2B and B2C studies equally. Historical studies coded before the magnitude layer existed (HR Leaders BGR, HR Leaders BambooHR, CEP &amp; Brand, JTBD Switch) remain as-is. Studies coded with the retired 1/2/3 intensity layer (UK Neobanks, Retail Banking CIBC, Intercom Fin, Retinol Switching) also remain as-is and carry <code class="inline-code">final_codes_with_intensity.json</code>; new studies write <code class="inline-code">final_codes_with_bws.json</code>. The magnitude layer is additive and BWS can be applied retroactively to frozen thematic coding if a follow-up analysis requires it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE CODING PROCESS -->
  <section class="section section-alt" id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>The coding process</h2>
      <p>
        Our thematic coding follows a four-step process grounded in established qualitative research methods. Each step has specific rules and quality checks.
      </p>

      <!-- PROCESS FLOW -->
      <div class="process-flow">
        <div class="process-step">
          <div class="step-number">1</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h3>Segment into meaning units</h3>
            <p>
              Each response is broken into discrete meaning units: the smallest segment of text that contains a single idea or claim (Graneheim &amp; Lundman, 2004). A participant who says three different things gets three separate meaning units, each coded independently.
            </p>
            <div class="step-example">
              <div class="step-example-label">Before segmentation</div>
              <div class="step-example-text">"The onboarding was clunky, we had to re-enter data in three places, and we were paying too much"</div>
              <div class="step-example-label">After segmentation</div>
              <div class="meaning-units">
                <div class="meaning-unit">
                  <span class="mu-num">MU-1</span>
                  <span class="mu-text">"The onboarding was clunky"</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meaning-unit">
                  <span class="mu-num">MU-2</span>
                  <span class="mu-text">"we had to re-enter data in three places"</span>
                </div>
                <div class="meaning-unit">
                  <span class="mu-num">MU-3</span>
                  <span class="mu-text">"we were paying too much"</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="process-connector">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 40" fill="none"><path d="M12 0 L12 32 M6 26 L12 32 L18 26" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </div>

        <div class="process-step">
          <div class="step-number">2</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h3>First-cycle coding</h3>
            <p>
              Each meaning unit receives a descriptive code: a short label (2-5 words) capturing what the statement is about (Saldana, 2016). Codes use the participant's own language where it is distinctive ("in vivo coding") and standardized labels where consistency matters.
            </p>
            <div class="step-example">
              <div class="code-assignments">
                <div class="code-assignment">
                  <span class="mu-text-sm">"The onboarding was clunky"</span>
                  <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
                  <span class="code-label">Poor usability</span>
                </div>
                <div class="code-assignment">
                  <span class="mu-text-sm">"re-enter data in three places"</span>
                  <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
                  <span class="code-label">Duplicate data entry</span>
                </div>
                <div class="code-assignment">
                  <span class="mu-text-sm">"paying too much"</span>
                  <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span>
                  <span class="code-label">Cost concerns</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="process-connector">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 40" fill="none"><path d="M12 0 L12 32 M6 26 L12 32 L18 26" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </div>

        <div class="process-step">
          <div class="step-number">3</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h3>Theme construction (two-pass approach)</h3>
            <p>
              Related codes are grouped into themes across all questions at once, not question by question. This is the critical design choice that ensures theme names are consistent across the full study. We use a two-pass approach (Deterding &amp; Waters, 2021):
            </p>
            <div class="two-pass">
              <div class="pass-card">
                <div class="pass-header pass-1">Phase 1: Discovery (5 phases, 5 agents)</div>
                <div class="pass-body">
                  Two extractors independently break responses into meaning units per question (in parallel). A clusterer compresses those units into rough semantic groups. A global synthesizer sees all clusters from all thematic questions in one call and names themes once for the entire study. A per-question validator applies the global codebook to each question and flags gaps. A human researcher reviews the final codebook before application begins.
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="pass-card">
                <div class="pass-header pass-2">Phase 2: Application (3 agents)</div>
                <div class="pass-body">
                  Three independent AI agents apply the validated codebook to all transcripts. Two coders with opposing biases code independently. A neutral arbiter (Claude Opus) resolves disagreements. Cohen's Kappa measures reliability per code.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-example">
              <div class="theme-grouping">
                <div class="theme-group">
                  <div class="theme-name">Theme: Product usability issues</div>
                  <div class="theme-codes">
                    <span class="code-pill-sm">Poor usability</span>
                    <span class="code-pill-sm">Confusing navigation</span>
                    <span class="code-pill-sm">Too many clicks</span>
                    <span class="code-pill-sm">Duplicate data entry</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="process-connector">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 40" fill="none"><path d="M12 0 L12 32 M6 26 L12 32 L18 26" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
        </div>

        <div class="process-step">
          <div class="step-number">4</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h3>Theme refinement</h3>
            <p>
              Candidate themes are tested against specific rules to ensure they are coherent, distinct, and analytically useful. Themes may be split, merged, or reorganized based on these checks.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THEME VALIDATION RULES -->
  <section class="section" id="validation">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Theme validation rules</h2>
      <p>
        Themes are not arbitrary groupings. Each must pass specific validation criteria before it enters the final analysis.
      </p>

      <div class="rules-grid">
        <!-- Minimum frequency -->
        <div class="rule-card">
          <div class="rule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none"><rect x="4" y="28" width="6" height="8" rx="1" fill="var(--border)"/><rect x="13" y="22" width="6" height="14" rx="1" fill="var(--border)"/><rect x="22" y="14" width="6" height="22" rx="1" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.5"/><rect x="31" y="6" width="6" height="30" rx="1" fill="var(--ink)"/><line x1="4" y1="27" x2="37" y2="27" stroke="#C75450" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>5% minimum frequency</h3>
          <p>
            A theme must be mentioned by at least 5% of participants to stand on its own. Themes below this threshold are merged with related themes or moved to "Other." This prevents findings from being driven by isolated comments.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- When to split -->
        <div class="rule-card">
          <div class="rule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none"><circle cx="20" cy="14" r="8" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="var(--card-bg)"/><circle cx="12" cy="30" r="6" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="var(--card-bg)"/><circle cx="28" cy="30" r="6" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="var(--card-bg)"/><line x1="16" y1="20" x2="12" y2="24" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="24" y1="20" x2="28" y2="24" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>Split when two concepts emerge</h3>
          <p>
            If the quotes within a theme cluster into two or more distinct ideas, the theme is too broad. A theme about "convenience" that contains both "close to my office" and "fast service" captures two different concepts and should be split for actionable analysis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- When to merge -->
        <div class="rule-card">
          <div class="rule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none"><circle cx="12" cy="14" r="6" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="var(--card-bg)"/><circle cx="28" cy="14" r="6" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="var(--card-bg)"/><circle cx="20" cy="30" r="8" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5" fill="var(--card-bg)"/><line x1="12" y1="20" x2="16" y2="24" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="28" y1="20" x2="24" y2="24" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>Merge at 70%+ participant overlap</h3>
          <p>
            When 70% or more of participants who mention Theme A also mention Theme B, the themes likely represent the same underlying concept. They are merged into a single theme to avoid double-counting and simplify the analysis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Theme count -->
        <div class="rule-card">
          <div class="rule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none"><rect x="4" y="6" width="32" height="5" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.2"/><rect x="4" y="13" width="32" height="5" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.3"/><rect x="4" y="20" width="32" height="5" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.5"/><rect x="4" y="27" width="32" height="5" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.7"/><rect x="4" y="34" width="20" height="3" rx="1.5" fill="var(--border)"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>Target 6-10 themes per question</h3>
          <p>
            Fewer than 4 themes for an open-ended question usually means important distinctions are being lost. More than 12 usually means themes are not abstracted enough. Sub-themes preserve nuance within the 6-10 target range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Other threshold -->
        <div class="rule-card">
          <div class="rule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none"><rect x="4" y="8" width="32" height="28" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="20" y="26" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="#C75450">15%</text><line x1="4" y1="8" x2="36" y2="8" stroke="#D4A843" stroke-width="3"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>"Other" capped at 15%</h3>
          <p>
            If the "Other" category exceeds 15% of responses, a meaningful pattern is being missed. The uncategorized responses are reviewed to identify hidden themes that should be added to the codebook.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Sub-themes -->
        <div class="rule-card">
          <div class="rule-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" fill="none"><rect x="2" y="4" width="36" height="8" rx="2" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.3"/><rect x="6" y="16" width="28" height="6" rx="1.5" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.5"/><rect x="6" y="24" width="22" height="6" rx="1.5" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.5"/><rect x="6" y="32" width="16" height="6" rx="1.5" fill="var(--ink)" opacity="0.5"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>Sub-themes preserve detail</h3>
          <p>
            Broad themes work for executive summaries. Sub-themes provide the detail needed for actionable recommendations. A theme like "Value for money" (21%) might contain sub-themes for "Low absolute prices" (14%), "Deals and promotions" (8%), and "Portion value" (5%).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8-AGENT SYSTEM -->
  <section class="section section-alt" id="agents">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>The two-phase, 9-agent system</h2>
      <p>
        Every study passes through a two-phase, nine-agent process. In Phase 1, six discovery agents work across six sequential phases to build a canonical codebook and segmentation dimension structure from all transcripts. In Phase 2, three application agents apply that codebook with built-in reliability measurement. No single agent's judgment is trusted in isolation, and a human review gate separates the two phases.
      </p>

      <!-- CROSS-QUESTION THEME CONSISTENCY -->
      <div class="subsection">
        <h3>The core validity problem: theme names coined per question</h3>
        <p>
          Earlier AI coding systems synthesized themes independently for each question. This creates a subtle but serious validity problem: the same underlying concept can get different names depending on which question surfaces it first. An agent analyzing Q5 ("Why did you switch?") might name a theme "Ease of use problems." The same agent, working independently on Q10 ("What frustrated you most?"), might name the same concept "Interface complexity" or "Faster processing" because it had no memory of what it had coined before.
        </p>
        <p>
          When that happens, findings become incomparable across questions. A researcher cannot sum "Ease of use problems" from Q5 with "Interface complexity" from Q10 without knowing they represent the same thing. The analysis appears fragmented, and cross-question patterns are invisible.
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          <h4>How the 5-phase design solves this</h4>
          <p>
            Braun and Clarke (2021) state that themes should be developed from the full dataset, not constructed within individual data items. Our Phase 4 global synthesizer does exactly this: it receives compressed clusters from every thematic question simultaneously and names themes once. Theme names coined in Phase 4 are used in Phase 5 validation for every question. "Ease of use problems" is the name for that concept everywhere in the study, regardless of which question triggered it.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is not just operational tidiness. Cross-question theme consistency is a validity requirement. Without it, percentage breakdowns across questions cannot be meaningfully compared, and study-wide frequency claims are indefensible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WHY 5 AGENTS + ACCURACY -->
      <div class="subsection">
        <h3>Why multiple independent agents?</h3>
        <p>
          A single AI coder, no matter how accurate, provides no way to measure reliability. The same data could be coded differently by a different system, and there would be no way to know which is correct. Multiple independent agents solve this by replicating the gold-standard practice of inter-rater reliability from human qualitative research (Cohen, 1960; Krippendorff, 2004), but without the time, cost, and fatigue limitations of human coders.
        </p>
        <p>
          Five agents use Claude Sonnet for high-volume extraction, per-question validation, and application coding. Three agents use Claude Opus: D3 (Preliminary Clusterer), D4 (Global Theme Architect), and Agent 3 (Resolver). Opus scores 17 points higher than Sonnet on expert reasoning benchmarks (GPQA Diamond: 91.3% vs. 74.1%). Each of these three agents makes decisions that either cannot be corrected downstream or that propagate into every subsequent step.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- WHY D3 AND D4 USE OPUS -->
      <div class="subsection">
        <h3>Why D3 and D4 use Opus: the irreversible information loss problem</h3>
        <p>
          D4 can only see what D3 passes it. D3 receives ~150 raw descriptive codes per question and compresses them into ~30 clusters. D4 receives those clusters — not the original codes. If D3 collapses two distinct concepts into one cluster, D4 has no way to recover the distinction. The information loss is permanent.
        </p>
        <p>
          This asymmetry drives the choice of Opus for D3. The failure modes are not symmetric:
        </p>
        <div class="two-pass" style="margin-top: 16px;">
          <div class="pass-card">
            <div class="pass-header pass-1">Under-clustering (recoverable)</div>
            <div class="pass-body">
              D3 keeps two concepts as separate clusters when they could have been merged. D4 sees both clusters, recognizes they represent the same underlying idea, and combines them into one theme. No information is lost. D4 is well-suited to this merging task because it sees cross-question context that D3 does not.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pass-card">
            <div class="pass-header pass-2" style="background:#C75450; color:white;">Over-merging (permanent)</div>
            <div class="pass-body">
              D3 collapses two distinct concepts into one cluster. D4 receives one cluster entry. D4 can only catch this if the representative quote reveals both concepts — if the quote is ambiguous, D4 names one theme and the distinction is gone. Example: "absolute price too high" and "poor ROI for what you paid" are different buying signals. A Sonnet D3 might merge them into "cost concerns." An Opus D3 keeps them separate and lets D4 decide whether to merge.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 20px;">
          <h4>D3 Opus persona: conservative by design</h4>
          <p>
            The D3 Opus persona is explicitly instructed: "When in doubt about whether two codes belong together, keep them separate. Under-clustering is recoverable downstream; over-merging is not." This is the opposite of the original Sonnet D3 persona, which was told to "err toward over-clustering." That instruction was wrong — it protected against under-clustering (the recoverable failure) while accepting over-merging (the permanent failure).
          </p>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 16px;">
          D4 uses Opus for a separate reason: it is the single most consequential call in the entire pipeline. Every theme name coined in Phase 4 is used in Phase 5 validation, in the final codebook, and in every application code assignment. Sonnet produces acceptable codebooks. Opus produces sharper conceptual distinctions, more precise definitions, and is less likely to conflate concepts that are related but analytically distinct.
        </p>
        <p>
          Combined, the upgrade adds approximately $2.56 per discovery run. For a $50K–$150K consulting engagement, this is noise. The protection against permanent information loss at D3 and poor theme naming at D4 is worth it unconditionally.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h3>Two ways to run the pipeline</h3>
